From 3f53d2c4cb8342825af20a66d096310bb1e0fddc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 23:05:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 144/209] feat: glare thresholds mean "stops above mid grey" on
 the stock path too; sky sources no longer starve the budget

1. Stock-path threshold. Off the switch the HDR target is raw scene radiance, so the stored
   threshold (4.0) meant 4.0 engine units = 40 000 cd/m2 - an absolute luminance, a different
   set of objects than the scene-referred path's "4 stops over grey", and one that did not
   move with the aperture. Now ResolveThreshold applies the same rule on both paths: N stops
   above the frame's own mid grey, where the stock mid grey is 0.18 / fExposure - the exact
   exposure FilmMapping applies that frame, read back (two-phase, non-blocking, 1x1) from the
   adapted-luminance texture by CAutoExposureStage::UpdateStockMidGreyReadback. Demand-latched:
   the readback exists only while one of our effects resolves a threshold (one second of
   hysteresis), so the camera-off pipeline is untouched; before the first measurement lands the
   old absolute reading is used. The wave-glare report prints the mid grey in force and every
   "stops" figure against it.

2. Sky de-prioritisation in the source budget: r_HDRDiffractionSkyWeight (default 0.25). A
   source at sky depth competes for a full-sprite slot with priority = excess x weight, so a
   lamp in the scene wins over sky texels of similar energy; the deposit is always the
   unweighted excess, so frame light is identical at every weight and a losing sky source
   still emits its one-texel energy quad. The sun disc survives by decades. Depth reaches the
   source pass through its own threshold technique (alpha = sky flag), kept through the
   pre-blur, and the priority expression is identical in the stats pass and the sprite VS.

decisions/s9-glare-stock-threshold.md; STRUCTURE.md; ConsoleReference.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
